{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "The clinical type/category of the patient's presenting concern (chief complaint) in each visit note",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "concern_category",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Directly operationalises the focus by classifying each visit's presenting concern into a coherent taxonomy of clinical concern types (Musculoskeletal, Cardiovascular, Mental Health, etc.), which is exactly the decomposition the query asks for."
    },
    {
      "name": "chief_complaint",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Extracts the specific presenting concern as free text, providing the granular basis from which the category is inferred. It is a coherent facet of the same focus, though highly granular and mostly unique rather than an analytical grouping."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.92,
  "structure_quality": 0.7,
  "focus_actionability": 0.85,
  "rationale": "Both added columns centre tightly on one coherent, data-inferable focus: the type of concern a patient presents with, which is precisely what the query targets. concern_category supplies a clean, useful-granularity categorical decomposition that directly answers 'main types of concerns.' chief_complaint adds the raw extracted concern, coherent with the focus but nearly one-value-per-row, so it functions more as source detail than as a distinct analytical facet, slightly weakening the structural differentiation (it is close to a restatement feeding the category). Still, the pair forms a sound extract-then-categorise structure. The implied focus is clearly actionable: an analyst can rank concern categories, cross-tabulate with department/urgency/age_group, and investigate high-frequency clusters. Structure_quality is held below the top because the second column adds limited independent analytical structure beyond the categorisation."
}
```

